The '''Extraterritorial Jurisdiction Act''' ({{nihongo|域外管轄権法|Iki-gai Kankatsuken Hō}}), commonly referred to as the '''EJA''' or the '''Naruse Law''' ({{nihongo|成瀬法|Naruse Hō}}), is a statute of [[Okaiken]] that establishes the circumstances under which Sanese courts may exercise criminal jurisdiction over conduct committed outside Sanese territory. The Act was enacted in 2005 in direct response to the case of [[Mizue Naruse]], the founder of the [[Hope and Survival Happiness Group]], who was acquitted of all charges by a Balboan appellate court in 2004 and returned to Okaiken, where she could not be prosecuted because all of her alleged criminal conduct had occurred in [[Balboa]].

Prior to the EJA, Okaiken's criminal law operated on a strict principle of territoriality: only conduct committed within Sanese territory was subject to Sanese criminal jurisdiction. This principle, inherited from the [[Saito Constitution]] era and reinforced by the postwar criminal code, meant that a Sanese national who committed serious offences abroad could return to Okaiken and face no legal consequences, provided that no independent offence had been committed on Sanese soil. The Naruse case exposed this gap in a way that generated sustained public controversy and cross-party support for reform.

The Act establishes three bases for extraterritorial jurisdiction: the '''active nationality principle''' (jurisdiction over Sanese nationals who commit serious offences abroad), the '''passive personality principle''' (jurisdiction over offences committed abroad against Sanese nationals), and the '''universal jurisdiction principle''' (jurisdiction over a limited category of offences recognised under international law as subject to universal jurisdiction). The Act also establishes procedural safeguards, including a requirement that the [[Minister of Justice (Okaiken)|Minister of Justice]] approve any extraterritorial prosecution and a prohibition on double jeopardy where the person has already been finally convicted or acquitted in the territory where the conduct occurred.

==Background==

===The territoriality principle in Sanese criminal law===

Okaiken's criminal code, enacted during the [[Saito Restoration]] and substantially reformed after the [[Great Adonian War]], was built on the principle of strict territoriality. Under this framework, Sanese courts had jurisdiction only over offences committed within the physical territory of Okaiken. The principle was consistent with the civil law tradition that Okaiken inherited from Breisland legal models during the modernisation of the late 19th century, and it was reinforced by the postwar constitutional order's emphasis on legal certainty and due process.

The territoriality principle had limited practical consequences for most of Okaiken's modern history. The vast majority of criminal cases involved domestic conduct, and the rare cases of serious offences committed abroad by Sanese nationals were typically handled by the foreign jurisdiction where the conduct occurred. Extradition, where necessary, was governed by bilateral treaties and the [[Immigration and Nationality Act (Okaiken)|Immigration and Nationality Act]].

However, the principle contained a structural gap: where a Sanese national committed offences abroad and then returned to Okaiken, and where the foreign jurisdiction either could not or did not successfully prosecute, the person faced no criminal liability in Okaiken. This gap was theoretical for most of the postwar period, because the combination of effective foreign prosecution and bilateral extradition treaties meant that serious offenders were typically tried in the jurisdiction where the conduct occurred.

===The Naruse case===

{{main|Mizue Naruse|Balboa v. Hope and Survival Happiness Group}}

The case of [[Mizue Naruse]] transformed the territoriality gap from a theoretical concern into a public controversy. Naruse, the founder of the [[Hope and Survival Happiness Group]], had been convicted in [[Balboa v. Hope and Survival Happiness Group|the Balboan criminal trial]] that followed the [[Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff|1999 armed standoff]] between the group and the Balboan military. She was deported to Okaiken after conviction.

In 2004, a Balboan appellate court acquitted Naruse of all charges, finding that the original trial had been substantially prejudiced by media coverage and that key prosecution evidence had been obtained through procedures that did not meet due process standards. Upon her return to Okaiken, the [[Ministry of Justice (Okaiken)|Ministry of Justice]] examined whether she could be prosecuted under Sanese law for the conduct that had formed the basis of the Balboan case. The conclusion was unequivocal: the alleged offences — theft, coercive confinement, illegal weapons possession — had all occurred in Balboa. No act committed within Okaiken's borders independently satisfied the elements of a criminal offence. Under the strict territoriality principle, Sanese courts had no jurisdiction. Naruse was released without charge.

The decision generated sustained public criticism. Editorialists, opposition politicians, and victims' advocates argued that a Sanese national who had founded a coercive organisation on Sanese soil and then exported it abroad should not escape accountability simply because the worst conduct occurred in another jurisdiction. The ''[[Toyozawa Mainichi]]'' editorialised that "the law's territoriality is a technicality, not a principle." The [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] opposition made the case a centrepiece of its criticism of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] government's criminal justice record.

===The civil law context===

The territoriality gap was particularly notable given Okaiken's position as a civil law jurisdiction. In civil law systems, extraterritorial jurisdiction is typically established by statute and applied broadly — the criminal code itself defines the scope of extraterritorial application, and the principle of active nationality jurisdiction (jurisdiction over nationals who commit offences abroad) is generally available for serious offences. In common law systems, by contrast, extraterritorial jurisdiction is typically limited to specific enumerated offences and requires express statutory authorisation for each category.

Okaiken's criminal code, despite its civil law heritage, had never been amended to include extraterritorial provisions. The omission was not deliberate — it was a consequence of the postwar code's focus on domestic reconstruction and the assumption that serious offences committed abroad would be handled by the territorial state. The Naruse case demonstrated that this assumption was not always valid.

==Legislative history==

===Introduction===

The Extraterritorial Jurisdiction Act was introduced to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] on 14 February 2005 by [[Yoshito Sengoku]] of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]], the governing party. The bill was co-sponsored by members of the [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] and the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]], reflecting the cross-party concern generated by the Naruse case. The [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] opposition, which had initially criticised the government's handling of the Naruse case, supported the bill while proposing amendments to narrow its scope and strengthen procedural safeguards.

===Passage through the Diet===

The bill's passage was relatively swift, given the cross-party consensus on the need for reform:

* '''First reading''': 14 February 2005 — Introduced and referred to the Judicial Affairs Committee
* '''Committee stage''': 15 February – 10 May 2005 — The most significant amendments concerned the scope of the active nationality principle. The LDP's original draft applied extraterritorial jurisdiction to all offences punishable by three years' imprisonment or more; the PPO opposition successfully narrowed this to offences punishable by five years or more, with a specific schedule of included offences. The SCP proposed an amendment adding universal jurisdiction for genocide, crimes against humanity, and war crimes; this was accepted. The OSP proposed a double jeopardy safeguard, which was incorporated as Section 5
* '''Second reading''': 11 May 2005 — Passed the House of Commoners
* '''Third reading''': 18 May 2005 — Passed 287–141. The [[House of Officers]] approved the bill on 25 May 2005
* '''Imperial Assent''': 8 June 2005 — The Act received Imperial Assent and took effect on 1 July 2005

===The "Naruse Law" designation===

The Act quickly became known as the '''Naruse Law''' ({{nihongo|成瀬法|Naruse Hō}}) in public discourse, a designation that reflected the specific case that had prompted its enactment. The name was used by both supporters and critics of the legislation. Supporters argued that the Naruse case had exposed a dangerous gap in the criminal code; critics argued that the law was a form of retrospective moral legislation that targeted a single individual who had already been acquitted. The Ministry of Justice has never formally adopted the "Naruse Law" designation, but it is widely used in legal commentary and media coverage.

==Key provisions==

===Section 1: Active nationality principle===

Section 1 establishes that Sanese courts have jurisdiction over a Sanese national who commits an offence outside Sanese territory, provided that:

:(a) The offence is listed in the Schedule of Extraterritorial Offences (Section 7)
:(b) The conduct would constitute an offence under Sanese law if committed within Sanese territory
:(c) The offence is punishable by imprisonment of five years or more under the law of the place where the conduct occurred, or the place where the conduct occurred does not have a functioning criminal justice system

The Schedule of Extraterritorial Offences includes: homicide, manslaughter, assault causing grievous bodily harm, kidnapping and false imprisonment, sexual offences, theft and robbery, fraud and embezzlement (where the amount exceeds 円10 million), arson, weapons offences, and offences involving organised criminal groups.

===Section 2: Passive personality principle===

Section 2 establishes that Sanese courts have jurisdiction over offences committed outside Sanese territory where the victim is a Sanese national, provided that:

:(a) The offence is listed in the Schedule of Extraterritorial Offences
:(b) The conduct would constitute an offence under Sanese law if committed within Sanese territory
:(c) The person accused of the offence is present in Okaiken or is a Sanese national

===Section 3: Universal jurisdiction===

Section 3 establishes that Sanese courts have jurisdiction over the following offences regardless of where the conduct occurred and regardless of the nationality of the perpetrator or the victim:

:(a) Genocide
:(b) Crimes against humanity
:(c) War crimes
:(d) Torture
:(e) Enforced disappearance

This provision was added at the proposal of the [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] during the committee stage and was accepted by the government as consistent with Okaiken's obligations under the [[Adonian Community]] charter and the [[Rome Statute]] framework.

===Section 4: Ministerial approval===

Section 4 provides that no prosecution may be initiated under Sections 1 or 2 without the written approval of the [[Minister of Justice (Okaiken)|Minister of Justice]]. The Minister must be satisfied that:

:(a) The prosecution is in the public interest
:(b) The person has not been finally convicted or acquitted in respect of the same conduct in the territory where it occurred (see Section 5)
:(c) Extradition to the territorial state is not practicable or has been refused
:(d) The evidence available is sufficient to establish a prima facie case

The ministerial approval requirement was introduced as a safeguard against politically motivated or vexatious prosecutions. It is consistent with the civil law tradition of prosecutorial discretion exercised under ministerial authority.

===Section 5: Double jeopardy safeguard===

Section 5 provides that a person who has been finally convicted or acquitted in respect of conduct in the territory where it occurred may not be prosecuted under the Act for the same conduct. This provision implements the principle of ''ne bis in idem'' (double jeopardy) and was specifically drafted in response to concerns that the Act could be used to re-prosecute individuals who had already been acquitted abroad — as Naruse had been.

The safeguard does not apply where the foreign acquittal was obtained through fraud, corruption, or where the foreign proceedings were designed to shield the person from criminal responsibility.

===Section 6: Retrospectivity===

Section 6 provides that the Act applies only to conduct committed after the date of commencement (1 July 2005). The provision was the subject of significant debate during the legislative process. Several LDP backbenchers argued that the Act should apply retrospectively to the Naruse case; the PPO and the SCP opposed retrospectivity on constitutional grounds, citing Article 39 of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]], which provides that "no person shall be held criminally liable for an act which was lawful at the time it was committed." Prime Minister Sengoku ultimately adopted the non-retroactive position, noting that retrospective criminal legislation would undermine the constitutional principles his government was seeking to uphold.

The practical consequence of Section 6 is that Mizue Naruse could not be prosecuted under the Act, because the conduct at issue occurred before 1 July 2005. This outcome was widely described as the "Naruse exception" — the law bore her name but could not reach her.

===Section 7: Schedule of Extraterritorial Offences===

Section 7 establishes the Schedule of Extraterritorial Offences, which may be amended by order of the Minister of Justice with the approval of the Judicial Affairs Committee of both Houses. The initial schedule included the offences listed in Section 1 above. The schedule has been amended three times since enactment:

* '''2009 amendment''': Added human trafficking and people smuggling
* '''2014 amendment''': Added terrorism offences and financing of terrorism
* '''2021 amendment''': Added cybercrime offences where the conduct causes serious harm in Okaiken

==Constitutional basis==

The Act draws its constitutional authority from Article 31 of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]], which provides that "no person shall be deprived of life or liberty, nor shall any other criminal penalty be imposed, except according to procedure established by law." The Act establishes the procedure by which extraterritorial jurisdiction may be exercised, consistent with the constitutional requirement that criminal penalties be imposed only through law.

The Act also relies on Article 41, which provides that "the Diet shall be the highest organ of state power, and shall be the sole law-making organ of the State." The Diet's authority to define the scope of criminal jurisdiction is an exercise of its legislative power under Article 41.

The non-retroactivity provision (Section 6) is consistent with Article 39, which provides that "no person shall be held criminally liable for an act which was lawful at the time it was committed."

==Reception and criticism==

===Support===

The Act was broadly supported by the legal profession, victims' advocates, and the editorial boards of major newspapers. The ''[[Toyozawa Mainichi]]'' described the Act as "a necessary correction to a law that allowed a Sanese national to escape accountability for conduct that would have been criminal if committed at home." The [[Sanese Bar Association]] endorsed the Act, noting that the territoriality principle had been an anomaly among civil law jurisdictions and that the Act brought Okaiken into line with the practice of comparable legal systems.

===Criticism===

The Act attracted criticism from two directions.

'''Civil liberties organisations''' argued that the ministerial approval requirement (Section 4) gave the Minister of Justice excessive discretion over the decision to prosecute, and that the absence of judicial review of the Minister's decision created a risk of politically motivated prosecutions. The [[Okaiken Civil Liberties Union]] proposed an amendment requiring that the Minister's decision be subject to review by the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken|Supreme Court]]; this was rejected during the committee stage on the grounds that prosecutorial discretion was a matter of executive, not judicial, function.

'''Defence lawyers and academic critics''' argued that the Act was effectively a "Naruse Law" — legislation prompted by a single case and designed to address a specific perceived injustice rather than a systemic problem. Professor [[Kenjiro Arisaka]] of the [[Toyozawa Institute of Technology]] wrote that "the Extraterritorial Jurisdiction Act is a sound law in search of a problem. The Naruse case was an anomaly, not a pattern. The territoriality principle served Okaiken well for over a century. The Act addresses a gap that may never arise again." Supporters countered that the gap was structural, not anecdotal, and that the absence of a mechanism for extraterritorial prosecution was a deficiency regardless of how often it arose.

===The "Naruse exception"===

The Act's non-retroactivity provision (Section 6) produced what commentators called the "Naruse exception": the law was enacted because of Mizue Naruse but could not be applied to her. The irony was widely noted. The ''Toyozawa Mainichi'' editorialised that "the Diet has closed the barn door. The horse is in Toyozawa, writing editorials." Naruse herself, in a 2006 interview with the ''Mainichi'', said: "They made a law with my name on it. They made sure it could not reach me. That is the most honest thing the Diet has ever done."

==Subsequent applications==

The Act has been invoked in a small number of cases since its enactment:

* '''2008''': The Act was used to prosecute a Sanese national who had committed fraud in [[Balisca]] and fled to Okaiken. The prosecution was approved by the Minister of Justice; the defendant was convicted in the [[Okami District Court]] and sentenced to four years' imprisonment. This was the first application of the Act.
* '''2013''': The Act was used to prosecute a Sanese national who had committed sexual offences in [[Yanbaru]]. The defendant was convicted in the [[Aizawa District Court]] and sentenced to eight years' imprisonment.
* '''2019''': The Act was considered for use in a case involving a Sanese national who had participated in a fraudulent investment scheme in [[Dagit]], but the Minister of Justice declined to approve the prosecution on the ground that extradition to Dagit was practicable and the Dagit courts were the appropriate forum.
* '''2022''': The universal jurisdiction provision (Section 3) was invoked for the first time in a case involving a foreign national present in Okaiken who was alleged to have committed torture in a conflict zone. The case is pending before the [[Okami High Court]].

